CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose) The purpose of this Decree is to provide for matters mandated by the Act on the Protection of Virtual Asset Users and matters necessary for the enforcement thereof.

Article 2 (Scope of exclusion from virtual assets) "Those prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 2(1)h of the Act on the Protection of Virtual Asset Users (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") means the following:
  1. Electronic bonds under subparagraph 16 of Article 2 of the Electronic Financial Transactions Act;
  2. Gift certificates issued by an issuer with a specified amount or quantity of goods or services stated therein, which are stored and used on mobile devices, such as mobile phones;
  3. Deposits and electronic certificates equivalent thereto, which are handled in electronic form through the network of the Bank of Korea under the Bank of Korea Act (hereafter in this subparagraph referred to as the “Bank of Korea”) by any of the following persons (referring to a network that issues and manages currency in electronic form issued by the Bank of Korea):
  a. A bank under the Banking Act (hereinafter referred to as “bank”);
  b. Nonghyup Bank established under the Agricultural Cooperatives Act;
  c. The Suhyup Bank under the Fisheries Cooperatives Act;
  d. The Industrial Bank of Korea established under the Industrial Bank of Korea Act;
  e. Korea Development Bank under the Korea Development Bank Act;
  4. Electronic certificates that exist uniquely and are not replaceable with other electronic certificates, such as electronic certificates the primary purpose of which is collection and electronic certificates the sole purpose of which is to confirm transactions between parties to a transaction; provided, this shall not apply to electronic certificates determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission, such as electronic certificates that may be used as a means of payment for specific goods or services;
  5. Other things determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission as having no risk of undermining the rights and interests of users.

Article 3 (Scope of transfer of virtual assets) “ Acts prescribed by Presidential Decree” in subparagraph 2c of Article 2 of the Act means acts of transferring virtual assets between users, between virtual asset service providers, or between a user and a virtual asset service provider, such as transmitting virtual assets from one virtual asset address to another virtual asset address (referring to a unique identification number electronically generated for the management of transfer records and custody details of virtual assets; hereinafter the same shall apply), for the purpose of conducting the business under that subparagraph.

Article 4 (Establishment and composition of Virtual Asset Committee) (1) The Virtual Asset Committee (hereinafter referred to as the "Committee") shall be established under the Financial Services Commission to provide advice on matters concerning policies and systems for the virtual asset market and virtual asset service providers under Article 5(1) of the Act.
  (2) The Committee shall be comprised of not more than 15 members, including 1 chairperson.
  (3) The Vice Chairperson of the Financial Services Commission shall serve as the chairperson of the Committee.
  (4) The members of the Committee shall be appointed or commissioned by the Chairperson of the Financial Services Commission, with due consideration given to gender, from among the following persons:
  1. Public officials of Grade III or higher of the Financial Services Commission or public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service;
  2. Public officials of Grade III or higher of a central administrative agency related to virtual assets or public officials belonging to the Senior Executive Service, who are designated by the head of the relevant agency;
  3. Persons who have served as a judge, prosecutor, or attorney-at-law for not less than 7 years;
  4. Persons who have served as an associate professor or higher at a school under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Higher Education Act for at least 7 years and who have expertise in fields related to virtual assets;
  5. Persons who have worked for not less than 3 years at an institution or organization related to virtual assets;
  6. Persons who have engaged in consumer protection for not less than 5 years;
  7. Persons who have obtained technical qualifications in the field of information security or information technology and have worked in the relevant field for not less than 5 years.
  (5) The term of office of members falling under paragraph (4)3 through 7 (hereinafter referred to as "non-government members") shall be 2 years, and they may be reappointed only once.
  (6) Even when the term of office under paragraph (5) expires, a non-government member may continue to perform their duties until a successor is commissioned.

Article 5 (Dismissal and removal of members) The Chairperson of the Financial Services Commission may dismiss or remove a member of the Committee from office if the member falls under any of the following:
  1. Where the member is unable to perform duties for an extended period due to mental or physical incapacity;
  2. Where the member commits misconduct in connection with his or her duties;
  3. Where the member is deemed unfit for office due to neglect of duties, misconduct, or any other reason;
  4. Where the member fails to recuse himself or herself despite falling under any ground for exclusion under the subparagraphs of Article 6(1);
  5. Where the member expresses that it is difficult to perform his or her duties.

Article 6 (Exclusion, challenge, and recusal of members) (1) Where a member of the Committee falls under any of the following, he or she shall be excluded from deliberation and resolution of the Committee:
  1. Where the member or his or her spouse or former spouse becomes a party to the relevant agenda item (if the party is a corporation, organization, etc., its executive officers shall be included; hereafter in this subparagraph and subparagraph 2, the same shall apply) or is a joint right holder or a joint obligor with a party to the relevant agenda item;
  2. If the member is a current or former relative of any party involved in the relevant agenda item;
  3. Where the member, or his or her spouse or former spouse, or a corporation or organization to which any of them belongs, has provided testimony, statements, advice, research, services, or appraisal with respect to the relevant agenda item;
  4. Where the member or a corporation or organization to which the member belongs is or was a representative or agent of a party to the relevant agenda item;
  5. Where the member serves as an officer or employee of a corporation or organization to which a party to the relevant agenda item belongs, or has served as such within the past 3 years.
  (2) A party may apply for a challenge against a member with the Committee if there are grounds for exclusion under paragraph (1) or other circumstances that make it difficult to expect fair deliberation and resolution from the member, and the Committee shall determine whether to grant the challenge by resolution. In such cases, the member subject to the application for challenge shall not participate in the resolution.
  (3) If a member falls under any ground for exclusion under paragraph (1), he or she shall voluntarily recuse themselves from the deliberation and resolution of the relevant agenda item.

Article 7 (Operation of Virtual Asset Committee) (1) The chairperson of the Committee shall represent the Committee and shall have overall control over its business affairs.
  (2) When the chairperson of the Committee is unable to perform his or her duties due to unavoidable circumstances, a member designated in advance by the chairperson shall perform those duties on his or her behalf.
  (3) Meetings of the Committee shall be convened with the attendance of a majority of the incumbent members, and resolutions shall be adopted by a majority vote of the members present.
  (4) Matters necessary for the operation of the Committee, other than those provided in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be determined by the chairperson of the Committee following a resolution of the Committee.

CHAPTER II PROTECTION OF USER ASSETS

Article 8 (Protection of deposits) (1) "Financial institutions recognized as reliable under Presidential Decree, such as banks under the Banking Act" in Article 6(1) of the Act means any of the following institutions:
  1. Banks;
  2. The Nonghyup Bank established under the Agricultural Cooperatives Act;
  3. The Suhyup Bank established under the Fisheries Cooperatives Act;
  4. The Industrial Bank of Korea under the Industrial Bank of Korea Act.
  (2) Where a virtual asset service provider deposits or entrusts users’ deposits (referring to money received from users in connection with the purchase and sale of virtual assets, brokerage thereof, or other business activities; hereinafter the same shall apply) with the institutions referred to in the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) (hereinafter referred to as the “administering institution”) separately from its proprietary property under Article 6(1) of the Act, it shall enter into a contract with such administering institution that includes the following matters:
  1. That the deposits shall be managed separately from the administering institution’s own property;
  2. That the deposits shall be operated in any of the following methods and the proceeds therefrom shall be paid to the virtual asset service provider:
  a. Purchase of government bonds or municipal bonds;
  b. Purchase of debt securities the payment of which is guaranteed by any of the following institutions:
  1) The Government or a local government;
  2) A bank or other financial company prescribed and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission;
  c. Other methods prescribed and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission that are unlikely to undermine the safe management of deposits;
  3. That if the virtual asset service provider falls under any of the subparagraphs of Article 6(4) of the Act, he or she shall immediately notify the administering institution of such fact and provide data necessary to make priority payment of deposits to users.
  (3) The amount of deposits that a virtual asset service provider must deposit or place in trust with an administering institution under Article 6(1) of the Act shall be 100/100 or more of the total amount of deposits for each user (referring to the amount obtained by subtracting the amount under subparagraph 2 from the amount under subparagraph 1 for each user; hereinafter the same shall apply):
  1. The aggregate of the following amount:
  a. Amounts deposited by a user for the sale, purchase, or other transactions of virtual assets;
  b. Amounts paid by a virtual asset service provider to a user, such as fees for the use of deposits;
  2. The amount of all expenses related to trading or other transactions of virtual assets conducted by users, such as fees.
  (4) Except as provided in paragraphs (2) and (3), detailed matters necessary for the deposit, trust, and management of deposits shall be determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 9 (Exceptions to prohibition on transfer of deposits or provision thereof as collateral) "Cases prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 6(3) of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Where a virtual asset service provider transfers deposits to a company that survives or is incorporated after a merger, as the virtual asset service provider is absorbed into another company or consolidates with another company;
  2. Where a virtual asset service provider transfers all or part of its business and transfers deposits to the transferee under the transfer contract;
  3. Other cases determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission as cases unlikely to undermine the rights and interests of users.

Article 10 (Methods and procedures for priority payment of deposits) (1) When an administering institution makes priority payments of deposits to users under Article 6(4) of the Act, it shall comply with the following:
  1. The amount to be paid to users shall be calculated by the following methods:
  a. It shall be limited to the total amount of deposits deposited or entrusted with the management agency as of the date on which any of the subparagraphs of Article 6(4) of the Act becomes applicable;
  b. It shall be calculated by multiplying the amount of deposits for each user by the ratio obtained by dividing the total amount of deposits deposited or entrusted with the administering institution by the total amount of deposits for each user; provided, where the total amount of deposits deposited or entrusted with the administering institution is equal to or greater than the total amount of deposits for each user, the full amount of deposits for each user shall be paid;
  2. The amount calculated pursuant to subparagraph 1 shall be paid in priority in accordance with the procedures prescribed in the following items:
  a. The administering institution shall verify data or information regarding deposits from users and the virtual asset service provider;
  b. The following matters shall be publicly announced in at least 2 daily newspapers and on the website of the administering institution, etc. within the period determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission:
  1) The timing and place of payment of deposits;
  2) Other matters related to the payment of deposits.
  (2) If the head of the Financial Intelligence Unit under Article 3 of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information (hereinafter referred to as the “Commissioner of the Financial Intelligence Unit”) revokes a report or an amended report of a virtual asset service provider under Article 7(4) of that Act, he or she shall notify the administering institution thereof.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraph (1), detailed matters necessary for the methods and procedures for priority payment of deposits shall be determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 11 (Custody of virtual assets) (1) A virtual asset service provider shall, pursuant to Article 7(3) of the Act, safely keep virtual assets equivalent to or exceeding the ratio determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission within the limit of not less than 70/100 of users’ virtual assets, by separating them from the Internet; provided, where hacking, breach of trust, discontinuation of business, or any other cause equivalent thereto has occurred or is likely to occur, such assets shall be stored in accordance with the ratio separately determined and notified to the virtual asset service provider by the Financial Services Commission.
  (2) “Security standards prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 7(4) of the Act means the following standards:
  1. To safely keep users’ virtual assets, a virtual asset service provider shall establish and implement operational guidelines including the following matters and disclose them on the website, etc. of the administering institution:
  a. Matters concerning risk management and accident prevention;
  b. Matters concerning a system for preventing conflicts of interest;
  c. Matters concerning prohibition of re-entrustment of virtual assets;
  d. Matters equivalent to those referred to in items a through c and necessary for the safe custody of virtual assets;
  2. The safety and security of the information system for custody of virtual assets shall be inspected and evaluated at least once a year in accordance with the standards determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission;
  3. All virtual assets entrusted by the virtual asset service provider shall be safely kept by separating them from the Internet;
  4. Other standards necessary for the safe custody of virtual assets, as determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 12 (Scope of insured events) “ Accidents prescribed by Presidential Decree, such as hacking or system failures” in Article 8 of the Act means any of the following:
  1. An event arising from the forgery or alteration of an access medium (referring to a means or information used in virtual asset transactions that falls under any of the items of subparagraph 10 of Article 2 of the Electronic Financial Transactions Act; hereafter in this Article the same shall apply);
  2. An event occurring in the course of electronic transmission or processing of information related to the execution of contracts concerning acts under any of the items of subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act, transaction instructions (referring to an instruction by a user to a virtual asset service provider to process the trading, exchange, transfer, or custody and management of virtual assets), or similar matters;
  3. An event caused by using an access medium obtained by deceptive or other improper means through intrusion into an information and communications network under Article 2(1)1 of the Act on Promotion of Information and Communications Network Utilization and Information Protection (hereinafter referred to as "information and communications network") or an electronic device such as a computer for virtual asset transactions;
  4. An event caused by an act of attacking an information and communications network for virtual asset transactions, a related information system, or a network that issues or manages virtual assets, by any of the following methods:
  a. Methods such as hacking, computer viruses, logic bombs, mail bombs, denial of service, or high-powered electromagnetic waves;
  b. Methods of installing programs or technical devices, etc., that enable access to an information and communications network by bypassing its normal protection or authentication procedures, in such network or a related information system;
  5. Other events determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 13 (Types of virtual asset transaction records) The types of virtual asset transaction records under Article 9(1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "virtual asset transaction records") shall be as follows:
  1. Records on the types of virtual assets;
  2. Records on the details of virtual asset transactions as follows:
  a. The user engaging in the virtual asset transaction;
  b. The counterparty to the virtual asset transaction;
  c. The date and time, type, quantity, and amount of the virtual asset transaction;
  d. Order information of the user [applicable only where the entity responsible for storing virtual asset transaction records is a virtual asset exchange (referring to a virtual asset service provider that opens and operates a virtual asset market; hereinafter the same shall apply)];
  e. Records of changes to the matters under items a through d resulting from changes to transaction instructions, etc.;
  3. Records on the virtual asset addresses through which virtual asset transactions are conducted;
  4. Records on the types of electronic devices, such as computers, used in virtual asset transactions (including information used to identify such devices), and access logs thereof;
  5. Records on the fact of, and the grounds for, blocking deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets under Article 11(2) of the Act;
  6. Records on the results of constant monitoring of, and measures taken in response to, abnormal transactions under Article 12(1) of the Act;
  7. Records on notifications, reports, and filings concerning matters related to unfair trading practices under Article 12(2) of the Act;
  8. Records on fees received by the virtual asset service provider as consideration for virtual asset transactions;
  9. Other records necessary to trace or search the details of virtual asset transactions or to verify and correct errors therein, as determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 14 (Retention and destruction of virtual asset transaction records) (1) With respect to the methods of retaining virtual asset transaction records under Article 9(2) of the Act, Article 12(3) and (4) of the Enforcement Decree of the Electronic Financial Transactions Act shall apply mutatis mutandis.
  (2) With respect to the procedures and methods for destroying virtual asset transaction records under Article 9(2) of the Act, Article 16 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act shall apply mutatis mutandis.
  (3) Except as provided in paragraphs (1) and (2), detailed matters necessary for the retention, destruction, etc., of virtual asset transaction records shall be determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

CHAPTER III REGULATION OF UNFAIR TRADE

Article 15 (Scope of material nonpublic information) “ Methods prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 10(1) of the Act means that a virtual asset service provider, a person issuing virtual assets (including a corporation; hereinafter the same shall apply), or a person delegated with disclosure authority therefrom discloses information by any of the following methods and the period or time specified in the relevant subparagraph elapses:
  1. Information published in at least two general daily newspapers or specialized daily newspapers in the economic field with nationwide distribution under the Act on the Promotion of Newspapers: 6 hours from 00:00 of the day following the date of publication; provided, where it is published in the form of an electronic publication under that Act, 6 hours from the time of publication;
  2. Information broadcast through terrestrial broadcasting that can be viewed nationwide among broadcasts under the Broadcasting Act: 6 hours from the time of broadcast;
  3. Information provided through Yonhap News Agency under the Act on Promotion of News Communications: 6 hours from the time of provision;
  4. Information disclosed by a virtual asset exchange through an electronic transmission medium established and operated by it: 6 hours from the time of disclosure; provided, where the time of disclosure falls between 18:00 on the date of disclosure and 03:00 on the following day based on Korea Standard Time, 9 hours from 00:00 of the following day;
  5. Information disclosed by a person issuing virtual assets or a person delegated with disclosure authority therefrom through a website or an electronic transmission medium that satisfies all of the following requirements: 1 day from the time of disclosure:
  a. It shall be accessible to the general public;
  b. It shall have continuously posted, for the most recent 6 months (or, where 6 months have not elapsed since the issuance of the virtual asset, from the date of issuance to the date of disclosure), information that may materially affect users’ reasonable investment decisions or the value of the relevant virtual asset, such as total issuance volume, distribution plans, or business plans;
  6. Information disclosed by methods determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission: upon the lapse of the period or time determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 16 (Scope of prohibition on trading in self-issued virtual assets) (1) “Persons in a special relationship prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 10(5) of the Act means specially related persons under Article 3(1) of the Enforcement Decree of the Act on Corporate Governance of Financial Companies (hereinafter referred to as "specially related person").
  (2) “Procedures and methods prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 10(5)2 of the Act means the following procedures and methods:
  1. Where a virtual asset service provider acquires virtual assets issued by itself or its specially related persons, it shall disclose the following matters without delay through the electronic transmission medium it has established and operates (limited to where the virtual asset service provider is a virtual asset exchange) and on its website, and shall submit a statement thereof to the Financial Services Commission on a quarterly basis:
  a. The following matters concerning the acquisition of virtual assets:
  1) The type, quantity, and amount of the virtual assets;
  2) The grounds for acquiring the virtual assets;
  3) The circumstances leading to the acquisition of the virtual assets and the fact that the compliance officer of the virtual asset service provider (referring to an auditor or a person equivalent thereto, if no compliance officer exists) has confirmed such circumstances;
  4) The relationship with the specially related persons;
  b. A plan for disposing of the virtual assets;
  2. Other methods and procedures determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission to prevent unfair trading practices or conflicts of interest with users.

Article 17 (Good cause for permitting blockage of deposit or withdrawal of virtual assets) “ Good cause prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 11(1) of the Act means any of the following cases:
  1. Where a deposit or withdrawal of virtual assets for a user cannot be normally performed due to any of the following reasons:
  a. Where a virtual asset information system (referring to an information system for virtual asset transactions and a network for issuing and managing virtual assets; hereinafter the same shall apply) is unavailable due to a system failure therein or due to maintenance or inspection thereof;
  b. Where an information and communications network for virtual asset transactions is unavailable due to a system failure therein or due to maintenance or inspection thereof;
  c. Where an information and communications network of an administering institution or a financial company, etc. that has opened real-name verified deposit and withdrawal accounts pursuant to Article 7(3)2 of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information is unavailable due to a system failure therein or due to maintenance or inspection thereof;
  d. Where a counterparty to a user’s virtual asset transaction is unable to normally process deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets due to a system failure or similar cause in the virtual asset information system used by such counterparty;
  e. Where a virtual asset exchange proceeds with procedures for termination of transaction support for the relevant virtual asset (referring to no longer conducting acts of selling, purchasing, or exchanging a specific virtual asset or brokering, arranging, or acting as an intermediary for such sale or exchange; hereinafter the same shall apply) (limited to cases of blocking deposits of the relevant virtual asset);
  f. Where a virtual asset service provider conducts substantive inspection work to reconcile the status of virtual assets it actually holds with those entrusted by users;
  g. Where a virtual asset service provider proceeds with procedures for terminating its business under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act due to ex officio cancellation under Article 7(4), compliance with an order for suspension of business under Article 7(5), or closure of business under the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information (limited to cases of blocking deposits of virtual assets);
  2. Where an accident under Article 12 has occurred or is clearly expected to occur with respect to any of the following persons, and there is an urgent need to block deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets for the protection of users and security:
  a. A virtual asset service provider;
  b. A person issuing virtual assets;
  3. Where deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets are blocked pursuant to a request or order of an administrative agency under the Act, the National Tax Collection Act, the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information, or other relevant statutes or regulations;
  4. Where it is necessary to block deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets to comply with relevant statutes, such as Articles 5, 5-2, 5-3, and 8 of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information;
  5. Where withdrawals of virtual assets are blocked under the terms and conditions of virtual asset transactions of a virtual asset service provider (hereinafter referred to as “virtual asset transaction terms and conditions”) that provide for the blocking of withdrawals of virtual assets for a period not exceeding 72 hours from the time such virtual assets are deposited, for the purpose of preventing telecommunications financial fraud under subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Special Act on the Prevention of Loss Caused by Telecommunications-Based Financial Fraud and Refund for Loss and refunding losses to victims thereof;
  6. Where, for the purpose of preventing criminal activities such as money laundering, there are reasonable grounds to suspect that a virtual asset transaction is related to illegal property, money laundering, or financing of public intimidation under subparagraphs 4 through 6 of Article 2 of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information, and deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets are blocked under the virtual asset transaction terms and conditions that provide for such blocking; in such cases, the period for blocking deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets shall be the minimum period necessary within the scope of 2 months; provided, if there are unavoidable grounds, such period may be extended up to twice;
  7. Other cases where blocking deposits or withdrawals of virtual assets is necessary for the protection of users and the establishment of a transparent and sound trading order in the virtual asset market, as determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 18 (Scope of abnormal transactions) “ Abnormal transactions, such as transactions in which the price or trading volume of virtual assets fluctuates abnormally, prescribed by Presidential Decree” in Article 12(1) of the Act means any transaction or act in the virtual asset market that is likely to violate the prohibitions on unfair trading practices under Article 10(1) through (5) of the Act and that falls under any of the following:
  1. Where the price or trading volume of virtual assets fluctuates abnormally;
  2. Where there are rumors or reports that may affect the price, etc. of virtual assets;
  3. Other cases where there is a risk of undermining a fair trading order in the virtual asset market.

CHAPTER IV SUPERVISION AND DISPOSITIONS

Article 19 (Supervision and inspection of virtual asset service providers) (1) The Financial Services Commission may formulate and implement an inspection plan to efficiently and systematically conduct inspections under Article 13(1) of the Act.
  (2) “Interested parties prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 13(2) of the Act means any of the following persons (including officers and employees thereof, in the case of a corporation):
  1. A person issuing virtual assets;
  2. A person developing or operating a virtual asset information system;
  3. An administering institution;
  4. Financial companies, etc. that have opened real-name verified deposit and withdrawal accounts under Article 7(3)2 of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information;
  5. A person entrusted with business by a virtual asset service provider or any person falling under any of subparagraphs 1 through 4;
  6. A person that has entered into a contract with a virtual asset service provider in relation to any act under the items of subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act and provides services;
  7. Other persons determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.
  (3) "Matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 13(2)7 of the Act means any of the following matters:
  1. Matters concerning the establishment and operation of a system for preventing conflicts of interest;
  2. Matters concerning restrictions on users’ transaction limits and the scale of virtual asset transactions;
  3. Matters concerning restrictions on virtual assets handled;
  4. Other matters determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission as necessary for the protection of users and the maintenance of a sound trading order.

Article 20 (Methods of requesting submission of data regarding unfair trading practices) (1) Where the Financial Services Commission requests a virtual asset service provider to submit data necessary for an investigation pursuant to Article 14(4) of the Act, it shall do so by a document that includes all of the following:
  1. The purpose of the investigation;
  2. The type of virtual assets subject to the investigation, the type of transactions, and the transaction period.
  (2) The Financial Services Commission may, pursuant to Article 14(6) of the Act, publicly disclose the following information and data through newspapers, broadcasting, or its website:
  1. The entity to which the person involved belongs and personal data of that person;
  2. Details of an illegal act and measures taken;
  3. Statistical data, such as investigation performance;
  4. Other matters determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission as necessary to prevent illegal acts of related persons.

Article 21 (Scope of persons subject to corrective orders) “ Interested parties prescribed by Presidential Decree” in the provisions, with the exception of the subparagraphs, of Article 15(1) of the Act means the persons falling under any of the subparagraphs of Article 19(2) (including officers and employees thereof, in the case of a corporation).

Article 22 (Criteria and procedures for imposing penalty surcharges) (1) Where the Financial Services Commission imposes a penalty surcharge under Article 17 of the Act, it shall comply with the following criteria:
  1. The amount of profits gained (including unrealized profits) or losses avoided from the violation under the main clause of Article 17(1) of the Act shall be calculated in accordance with Appendix 1;
  2. The degree of the violation shall be determined by comprehensively taking into account the following:
  a. The amount of profits gained (including unrealized profits) or losses avoided from transactions related to the violation;
  b. The impact of the violation on market quotations or prices;
  c. Whether the violation falls under subparagraph 3 or 4;
  d. Other factors, such as the motive, nature, and duration of the violation;
  3. Where the violation has continued for at least one year or has been repeatedly committed at least 3 times within a period of 5 years, not less than 50/100 of the statutory maximum shall be imposed as a penalty surcharge;
  4. Where a violation falls under any of the following items, a penalty surcharge shall be reduced or exempted:
  a. Where the violation is deemed to have resulted from minor negligence or error;
  b. Where the violation has been corrected without delay;
  c. Where damages to users caused by the violation have been compensated.
  (2) Where the Financial Services Commission imposes a penalty surcharge pursuant to Article 17(1) of the Act, it shall notify the person liable, in writing, to pay the penalty surcharge, specifying the type of violation and the amount thereof, in accordance with the methods determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission; in such cases, except in any of the following cases, such notification shall be given after confirming the results of investigation or disposition regarding violations under Article 10(1) through (4) of the Act with the Prosecutor General: <Amended on Apr. 22, 2025>
  1. Where the Financial Services Commission has consulted with the Prosecutor General to impose a penalty surcharge after notifying or filing a complaint with an investigative agency;
  2. Where the results of the investigation and disposition cannot be confirmed even after 1 year has passed since the Financial Services Commission notified or filed a complaint with an investigative agency; provided, this shall not apply to cases where the Prosecutor General requests that the penalty surcharge be imposed after confirming the results of the investigation and disposition for any of the following reasons:
  a. Where there is a reasonable ground to delay the confirmation of the results of investigation or disposition, such as suspension of prosecution, etc.;
  b. Where imposing a penalty surcharge without confirming the results of investigation or disposition is likely to conflict with the results of investigation or disposition.
  (3) A person who receives a notice under paragraph (2) shall pay the penalty surcharge to a receiving institution determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission within 60 days from the date on which the person receives such notice.
  (4) Detailed matters necessary for imposing penalty surcharges other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3) shall be determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 23 (Delegation of authority) (1) The Financial Services Commission shall delegate the following duties to the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service (referring to the Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service established under Article 24 (1) of the Act on the Establishment of Financial Services Commission; hereinafter the same shall apply) in accordance with Article 18 of the Act:
  1. Receipt of reports under Article 11(2) of the Act;
  2. Inspections of the business and financial status of virtual asset service providers under Article 13(1) of the Act, and requests under Article 13(3) of the Act for reports, submission of data, appearance of witnesses, testimony, and statements of opinion;
  3. Orders to submit reports or data under Article 14(1) of the Act, requests for matters under the subparagraphs of Article 14(2) of the Act, and requests for submission of data under Article 14(4) of the Act; provided, this shall not apply where prompt handling is required for the protection of users and the maintenance of a sound trading order, as determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission;
  4. Warnings and cautions under Article 15(1)2 and 3 of the Act, requests for suspension of employees from duty under Article 15(2)2 of the Act, and cautions, warnings, or requests for disciplinary action against officers and employees under Article 15(2)3 of the Act; provided, this shall not apply where prompt handling is required for the protection of users and the maintenance of a sound trading order, as determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission;
  5. Receipt of statements under the provisions, with the exception of the items, of Article 16(2)1 of this Decree;
  6. Establishment of inspection plans under Article 19(1).
  (2) The Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service shall report to the Financial Services Commission on the details of the processing of the duties delegated under paragraph (1) every 6 months; provided, with respect to duties determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission, such report shall be made at the time determined and publicly notified by the Financial Services Commission.

Article 24 (Mutual notification of inspection plans) The Governor of the Financial Supervisory Service and the Commissioner of the Korea Financial Intelligence Unit may, in order to prevent duplication of inspections of virtual asset service providers, mutually notify each other of inspection plans under Article 19(1) and inspection plans for conducting inspections under Article 15(1) of the Act on Reporting and Using Specified Financial Transaction Information.

Article 25 (Processing of sensitive information and unique identification information) (1) The Financial Services Commission (including any person to whom the authority of the Financial Services Commission has been delegated under Article 23(1)) may, where unavoidable for performing the following affairs, process data containing information corresponding to criminal history records under subparagraph 2 of Article 18 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act and personal identification numbers under subparagraph 1-2a2) of Article 2 of the Credit Information Use and Protection Act (hereinafter referred to as "personal identification numbers"):
  1. Business affairs regarding the notification, report, and filing of matters related to unfair trading practices under Article 12(2) of the Act;
  2. Business affairs regarding the supervision, inspection, and measures against virtual asset service providers under Article 13 of the Act;
  3. Business affairs regarding the reporting, investigation, requests, measures, and public disclosure under Article 14 of the Act;
  4. Business affairs regarding measures under Article 15 of the Act;
  5. Business affairs regarding the imposition and collection of penalty surcharges under Article 17 of the Act;
  6. Business affairs regarding the imposition and collection of administrative fines under Article 22 of the Act.
  (2) A virtual asset service provider may, where unavoidable for performing the following duties, process data containing personal identification numbers:
  1. Business affairs regarding the acts under the items of subparagraph 2 of Article 2 of the Act;
  2. Business affairs regarding the management of deposits under Article 6(1) of the Act and the transfer or provision of deposits as collateral under Article 6(3) of the Act;
  3. Business affairs regarding the generation, preservation, and destruction of virtual asset transaction records under Article 9(1) of the Act;
  4. Business affairs regarding the constant monitoring of and measures against abnormal transactions under Article 12(1) of the Act, and the notification, report, and filing of matters related to unfair trading practices under Article 12(2) of the Act;
  5. Business affairs regarding the submission of data under Articles 13(3) and 14(4) of the Act.
  (3) An administering institution may, where unavoidable for performing business affairs regarding the payment of deposited or entrusted deposits under Article 6(4) of the Act, process data containing personal identification numbers.
  (4) An institution that keeps virtual assets of users under Article 7(4) of the Act may, where unavoidable for performing business affairs regarding the custody of virtual assets, process data containing personal identification numbers.

Article 26 (Re-examination of regulation) The Financial Services Commission shall review the feasibility of the following matters every 3 years starting from January 1, 2024 (referring to the period before the date identical to the reference date every third year) and take measures such as making improvements:
  1. Methods of depositing and managing deposits in trust pursuant to Article 8;
  2. Scope of exceptions to the prohibition on transfer or pledging of funds as collateral under Article 9;
  3. Methods and procedures for priority payment of deposits pursuant to Article 10;
  4. Custody ratios for virtual assets and applicable security standards under Article 11;
  5. Scope of insured events under Article 12.

CHAPTER V PENALTY PROVISIONS

Article 27 (Methods for calculating unjust enrichment) The specific methods for calculating the amount of profit gained (including unrealized profit) or loss avoided resulting from a violation under Article 19(1) and (2) of the Act shall be as set forth in Appendix 1.

Article 28 (Criteria for imposition of administrative fines) The criteria for the imposition of administrative fines under Article 22(1) of the Act shall be as set forth in Appendix 2.

